# Profile: autoheal
# A wedged ARQ worker (event loop alive but no heartbeat) is restarted by the autoheal sidecar, then takes work again.
# Product defaults are never changed by this file; it is the gate's input for the THROWAWAY stack only.
#
# requires: compose
# services: autoheal
# The `autoheal` compose service (its docker-socket-proxy comes up as its dependency) must run in the SAME compose
# project as the stack, and the stack must carry the project-scoped label key
# `com.neuralscape.autoheal.<compose project name>=true` on the workers (the sidecar only sees its own project).
#
# DESTRUCTIVE: the scenario wedges the fast worker on purpose and waits for the restart (about 10 minutes). It runs only
# with NS_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE=1 on a disposable compose project, in the gate's destructive pass:
#   -k "test_fault_injection or test_backup_restore"   (see acceptance/README.md, "Autoheal")
#
# The stall file lives under /dev/shm: that tmpfs is re-created empty whenever the container restarts, so a stall that
# outlives a restart cannot wedge the restarted worker again. The scenario also deletes it itself, in a finally.
NS_TEST_STALL_HEARTBEAT_FILE=/dev/shm/ns-test-stall-hb
# Only needed when the stack's own env sets NEURALSCAPE_PUBLIC_URL: the worker then refuses the stall hook without it.
NS_TEST_HOOKS_ALLOW_WITH_PUBLIC_URL=1
